# GENERATED SUITE ARTIFACTS -- never merged, always regenerated.
#
# The four paths below are pure functions of the tree. Nothing about them is
# authored: `scripts/reprobuild_suite_inventory.py` and
# `scripts/generate_test_edges.nim` derive every byte from the test sources
# the tree contains. Their correct post-merge value is therefore a function
# of the MERGED TREE, not of the two edits -- so a textual three-way merge of
# them is not a resolution, it is a fabrication that happens to parse.
#
# `-merge` makes git refuse rather than guess: when both sides changed one of
# these relative to the merge base, the path is left unmerged (`UU`) and the
# operation stops. Regenerate, do not resolve; see `just regen-suite-artifacts`.
#
# WHY `-merge` AND NOT A CUSTOM MERGE DRIVER. A driver (`merge=<name>` here
# plus `merge.<name>.driver` in config) can regenerate instead of merging, but
# `merge.<name>.driver` lives in `.git/config`, which is not versioned, and
# git offers no way to require it. Measured in a throwaway repo on git 2.54.0:
# with `merge=` declared here and no driver registered, git prints
# "Auto-merging ..." / "Merge made by the 'ort' strategy", exits 0, and
# produces the same silently wrong artifact as no protection at all -- no
# warning, no exit code, nothing to notice. A protection whose absence is
# invisible is worse than a loud conflict. `-merge` needs no out-of-band
# config, travels with every clone and worktree, and cannot fail open.
# Regeneration is also not affordable at merge time: `--write-inventory-sources`
# is a single-threaded source scan over every declared test source, measured at
# 4-7 minutes on this tree, and `--write-static-case-counts` adds ~2 more. A
# driver that paid that on every merge would be turned off inside a week.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT A CONFLICT ON EVERY INTEGRATION. `-merge` changes nothing
# unless BOTH sides moved the file differently: a one-sided change still
# resolves trivially, and two sides that produced identical bytes still
# resolve trivially. Measured over the 182 two-parent merges in the last 795
# commits of this repository: 51 of them (28%) had two-sided changes to one of
# these files. In 43 of the 51 EVERY two-sided artifact came out matching
# NEITHER parent -- a document assembled by git, which no generator ever
# emitted. In the other 8 at least one artifact came out byte-identical to one
# parent while its siblings were blended, which is worse rather than better:
# that is precisely how the artifacts end up contradicting each other, and
# those 8 can be shown wrong from the commit alone -- they disagree about
# which test sources exist, by one to four sources. The rest cannot be settled
# without regenerating a historical tree, which is the whole problem -- there
# is nothing at the merge to notice them. Those 51 are exactly the merges this
# line now stops.
#
# `-merge` does NOT imply `-diff`: these stay plain text in review, and git
# leaves OUR content in the working tree with no conflict markers, so the
# checkout the regeneration command runs against is coherent Nim and valid
# JSON rather than a file with `<<<<<<<` in it. Git words the refusal
# "warning: Cannot merge binary files: <path>" -- that is how it describes any
# path it was told not to merge, not a claim that the file became binary;
# `git diff` still renders it line by line.
#
# WHAT STILL GETS PAST IT, measured and deliberately left open. `git merge -X
# ours` / `-X theirs` exit 0 on an unmergeable path and take the named side
# ENTIRE -- a stale but internally coherent artifact. ONE gate catches that,
# not four: `--check-declared-sources` is the only one that walks the TREE, so
# it sees the test source the discarded side added; the other three derive
# their universe from repro_tests.nim, which `-X` made stale in the same
# direction, so all three agree with each other and pass. And that one gate is
# Nim-only (`_is_test_shaped_source` requires a `.nim` suffix), so a `-X`
# resolution that drops a PYTHON test source is caught by nothing here. Use
# `just regen-suite-artifacts`, not `-X`.
# `git merge --strategy=resolve` ignores the mark altogether and blends
# the two sides; the default strategy is `ort`, which honours it, so that is
# reachable only by naming a legacy strategy. Do not.
#
# Adding a fifth such artifact? `tests/unit/test_generated_artifact_merge_policy.py`
# derives this list from the generators' own path constants and fails if a
# generated artifact is missing a `-merge` mark.
# ANCHORED with a leading slash. A git attribute pattern with no slash in it
# matches that basename at ANY depth, so a bare `repro_tests.nim` would also
# claim a future `libs/<lib>/repro_tests.nim` that no generator writes. The
# three paths below carry a slash already and are anchored by that.
/repro_tests.nim -merge
scripts/reprobuild-test-shape-parity.tsv -merge
scripts/reprobuild-suite-static-case-counts.tsv -merge
benchmarks/reports/reprobuild-suite-m0-inventory-sources.json -merge

# Line endings for shell payloads that are executed on a Linux target
# regardless of the checkout's platform.
recipes/cache/*.sh text eol=lf
recipes/cache/systemd-units/* text eol=lf
tools/bootstrap-linux-smoke.sh text eol=lf
